### 1. Overall Sentiment

The dominant sentiment toward Software Engineering (SWE) as a career right now is **mixed and fragmented**—often best described as **cautiously optimistic about long-term relevance, but anxious in the short term**.

A major driver of the anxious tone is the perception that **the hiring market has cooled meaningfully compared to the pandemic-era peak**, with many people (especially early-career folks) describing the market as “hard mode.” That perception is supported by broad labor-market indicators showing **software development job postings sitting below pre-pandemic baseline levels**, implying weaker aggregate demand than many candidates expect. ([FRED][1])

At the same time, professional discourse remains **resilient** in one key way: most people still view software as a core capability in nearly every industry, and the developer ecosystem continues to grow in activity and participation. This creates a split tone: “software isn’t going away” coexists with “getting hired (or staying hired) feels less predictable.” ([The GitHub Blog][2])

Finally, the “AI” factor has shifted the emotional register from straightforward optimism to **pragmatic uncertainty**: AI tool usage is widespread, but enthusiasm has cooled as real-world limitations and risks are encountered. ([Stack Overflow][3])

